From 773e1bef6073a52d91b27eb7e660afcf54d95c0c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dgabehar <dgabehar@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 08:07:23 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] common : split-balance: calibrate on the model's own layers

The synthetic calibration (one dense matmul of the dominant shape) mis-predicted MoE
and small models badly, e.g. tg 153 predicted vs 56 measured on K2-Horizon-7B.
Time the real layer kinds instead:

- every repeating layer kind the model has (hybrid models have several), with its
  real weight shapes and types, the real expert count via mul_mat_id, flash
  attention at the workload's context, and GDN (ssm_conv + gated_delta_net)
- a slice of the output layer, which decode runs once per token on the last device
- the layer's other ops (norms, RoPE, elementwise) charged per node, using the
  compute node count of the real graphs from the fit probe
  (new llama_graph_n_compute_nodes) and a measured per-op cost
- the fixed cost of a graph run + readback is measured and subtracted

Busy nodes: after a warm-up pass each timing is repeated up to 4 rounds and the
fastest kept (other load only slows a node down); a large spread is logged.
quick_check takes the best of 3 tries.

Predicted vs measured on an RTX 3080 Ti, tg: gpt-oss-20b 89/103, Qwen3-1.7B
209/192, K2-Horizon-7B 60/56, Qwen3.5-4B 85/100.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
